    {
      "point": "Both list Axieros as one of the deities.",
      "quote_britannica": "the fests go are laid to be, in number, four, viz. Axieros, Axiocerla, Axiocerfius, and Calinila",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Axierus and Axiocersa for the pair of females"
    }
  ],
  "differences": [
    {
      "point": "Britannica calls them gods of the ancient Pagars; Wikipedia calls them enigmatic chthonic deities.",
      "quote_britannica": "a term in the biology of the ancient Pagars, figuring great and powerful gods",
      "quote_wikipedia": "were a group of enigmatic chthonic deities"
    },
    {
      "point": "Britannica names four specific gods; Wikipedia says the number varies and lists different common names.",
      "quote_britannica": "the fests go are laid to be, in number, four, viz. Axieros, Axiocerla, Axiocerfius, and Calinila",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The number of Cabeiri also varies, with some accounts citing four (often a pair of males and a pair of females)"
    },
    {
      "point": "Britannica mentions festivals called cabinets; Wikipedia describes a nine-day festival with extinguished fires.",
      "quote_britannica": "where the cabinets were celebrated in honour of them",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Lemnos held an annual festival of the Cabeiri, lasting nine days, during which all the fires were extinguished"
    },
    {
      "point": "Britannica gives no etymology; Wikipedia discusses Semitic, Hittite, and pre-Greek origins.",
      "quote_britannica": "CABIRI, a term in the biology of the ancient Pagars",
      "quote_wikipedia": "In the past, the Semitic word kabir (\"great\") has been compared to Κάβειροι since at least Joseph Justus Scaliger"
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Cabiri is a name given to the gods of Samothrace.",
